nt of ports to ship  lng from is as important as the  development of the  ports to actually receive lng and of  course germany doesn't have own  terminals so far but there is a  construction going on of other terminals  in other eu countries and  in a network um like it is in the eu  there's of course also the possibility  for germany to get liquefied gas through  other ports  karen patel  director of the efo center for energy  climate and resources thank you for your  insights  you for having me  western countries want to become  independent of russian energy supplies  that's the idea but what are the  alternatives russian hard coal could be  replaced by coal from indonesia  while more demand for the country's  industry is certainly welcome  indonesia's government does have its own  concerns  at the beginning of the year the  indonesian government cracked down  imposing an export ban on coal but the  ban was only in force until the end of  january now coal ships are allowed to  head for international ports again as  long as domestic supplies are short a  quarter of the production must remain in  indonesia sold at prices set by the  government  exported coal on the other hand will  reap a bonanza for the coal companies  thanks to the sharp rise in prices on  the world market fueled by the russian  war of aggression in ukraine the price  of a ton of coal recently climbed to  over 400 us dollars  but coal rich indonesia is also focusing  on renewable energies for the long run  and wants to be carbon neutral by 2060.  indonesia  as a tropical country yeah we are  having a lot of sources of solar  and also beside that we have also wind  and then there also marine  energy source  we beside those  beside those technology we also expect  the development rapid development of the  energy storage system  at the moment however  climate-damaging coal is the country's  most important energy source for this  year the government expects production  to grow by eight percent  well f